title: Testing the GUI end to end
tags: Development
kind: guide
audience: dev
aliases: gui-e2e
summary: scripts/gui-e2e.sh runs the GUI's commands in a real window against a throwaway daemon, and checks what they said — never what they look like.

The Rust tests of the GUI drive its orchestration against a stub daemon, and the vitest
suites its JavaScript under jsdom ([[Testing conventions]]). Neither reaches the chain
between them: the shell's dispatcher, the Tauri `invoke`, the `#[tauri::command]`
wrapper. A command misnamed in the shell, or an argument the wrapper never receives,
passes both. `scripts/gui-e2e.sh` runs that chain for real:

```
scripts/gui-e2e.sh [--unsandboxed] [--keep-logs] [scenario...]
```

It boots an isolated daemon — the fixture the shipped scripts' integration tests use:
private token directory, empty configuration, ephemeral port — and a real
`metafolder-gui` on top of it, then gives each scenario a repository of its own, shown
in a fresh focused workspace. A scenario types commands with [[mf gui command]] and
reads back the outcome with [[mf gui status-bar]] (kind and text of the last status
message) and [[mf gui messages]] (the message log).

!! Its own configuration

The GUI reads a private copy of the installed configuration (`XDG_CONFIG_HOME` under
the work directory; the rendered docs are only linked), so a user's edits — a
keybinding, a user command — cannot change an outcome, and a scenario can install
what it needs: the `script` scenario's `e2e-ask.sh`, which asks one key with
[[mf gui input]]. What the GUI runs finds the run's own `mf` first on its `PATH`, and
this GUI through `METAFOLDER_GUI_URL`.

A command that runs a script (`script:run`) resolves when the script ends, so the
scenario starts it in the background and answers meanwhile; what the script says may
land just after the command resolved, and is waited for (`until_ok`), never assumed.

!! What is asserted

What a command ''said'', not how the window shows it: no pixel, no DOM, no panel
layout. A change of style never breaks a scenario; a change of wording or of
behaviour does, which is the point.

Before each command the script posts a marker message of its own. A command that
reports nothing leaves the marker on the bar, so it fails instead of passing on the
message of the command before it.

!! Running it

It needs the binaries and the frontend built ([[Building]]), the configuration
installed (the GUI reads its panels from it — [[Updating the configuration]]), and a
display. `--unsandboxed` passes `--allow-unsandboxed-webview`, for a container that
forbids WebKit's sandbox ([[Sandboxing the media decoders]]). A run takes about ten
seconds, most of it the GUI starting. It is not part of `scripts/check.sh`, which runs
without a display. `METAFOLDER_BIN_DIR` (default `target/debug`) names the directory of
the binaries; [[Measuring coverage]] points it at instrumented ones, and the run ends
by quitting the GUI through its `quit` command — never a kill — so that their profiles
get written.

!! Confirmations

A confirmed command (`metarecord:trash`, `orphan:delete`, an `ignore:add` that would
shadow inherited patterns) is driven with `answering yes|no <command…>`, which passes
`--confirm` to [[mf gui command]] and keeps the questions asked in `LAST_QUESTIONS`. A
plain `run` answers `no` as well, and fails if anything was asked: a dialog nobody
answers blocks the shell's dispatcher, and every command after it would time out.

!! Adding a scenario

A `scenario_<name>` function: `new_repo <name>` for the repository and the workspace,
`df_mf` to prepare data through the daemon, `run` or `answering` to type a command,
then `status_is <name> <kind> <glob>`, `command_ok`, or the `assert_*` of
`scripts/lib/assert.sh`. `gui_status <python expression>` evaluates one over [[mf gui status]] (`ws_count`, `slot_ws <slot>` are built on it). `select_path <abs path>` makes a path's metarecord the
selection (through the `file` panel); `show_panel <slot> <type> <command>` shows a
panel and waits until one of its commands answers. Add the name to the default list
at the top.

Name the target of a command that acts on the selection, even when the default would
do: the workspaces show the `message` panel because the metarecord list selects a
record of its own once loaded, at a moment no scenario controls.

!! Not covered

The `sync` panel's conflict editor: a resolution is chosen in a row of the panel and
saved with its button, which no command reaches. The `sync` scenario runs a plan
without conflicts.
